fact WorkItem(id: "WI-1125", description: some(value: "A carrier-own `neq` is an ACCEPTED override that NOTHING DISPATCHES — make it dispatchable across the index, the resolver and eval, or decide it is not an override point and refuse it. FOUND BY: the WI-755 review, then measured three ways. THE SHAPE: a sort declaring `operation neq(a: C, b: C) -> Bool` and no own `eq`. It is legal and deliberately so — WI-1098/WI-999 accept it as an OVERRIDE, pinned by wi1098_derive_eq_total_test::declaring_neq_on_a_derived_carrier_is_an_override_not_a_capture (tests/include/wi1098_derive_eq_total_test.rs:624), whose comment says in as many words 'a derived provides PartialEq makes an neq declaration an OVERRIDE'. WHAT HONOURS IT: nothing. load.rs build_eq_dispatch_index keys ONLY `anthill.prelude.PartialEq.eq` suppliers (EqDispatchIndex::build reads eq_spec/eq_short; candidates() calls carrier_own_op(kb, carrier, self.eq_spec, self.eq_short)), so such a carrier's constructors key no entry; resolve.rs sem_eq_dispatch_target therefore finds no target and value_reaches_eq_override finds nothing buried, and sem_eq_values falls through to outcome 6, the STRUCTURAL verdict — against the carrier's own inequality. eval::builtins is blind identically: builtin_neq is `!semantic_equal(..)` and semantic_equal reads the same index. So the declaration is accepted, documented as an override, and silently ignored by every evaluator. MEASURED (probe over the stdlib + a Color carrier whose ONLY member is `operation neq(a,b) = false`, i.e. nothing unequal so everything is equal): (1) GUARD path, `effects { Boom :- eq(c, Red) }` at risky(Green) — on HEAD before WI-755 the WI-573 blanket gate KEPT Boom; with WI-755's gate removed outright it DROPPED it (a silent wrong answer); WI-755 therefore kept a narrowed floor, typing::guard_conjunct_reaches_undispatchable_neq, and rows neq_only_override_keeps_effect + nested_neq_only_override_keeps_effect pin it. (2) PROOF path, `requires neq(c, Red)` at needy(Green) — PROVES on HEAD too, i.e. this half was ALREADY WRONG before WI-755 and is what this ticket owns: WI-756 covered the proof paths but every one of its fixtures uses an `eq` override, so nothing reached the neq-only shape. The contract-`ensures` and in-body-`proof` sites resolve through the same builtin and are wrong the same way; census them rather than assuming the two named here are all. SCOPE — decide the design question FIRST, because the two answers are opposite: (a) `neq` IS an override point: EqDispatchIndex must key a carrier-own `neq` and carry the POLARITY (functor -> Symbol becomes functor -> (Symbol, is_neq)), sem_eq_dispatch must invert the verdict for a neq target, eval::builtins::semantic_equal must do the identical thing (resolver/interpreter/codegen agreement is the WI-645 invariant), the AmbiguousEqDispatch refusal must treat an eq supplier and a neq supplier for one carrier as a conflict, and cpp codegen needs a look; THEN retire typing::guard_conjunct_reaches_undispatchable_neq, whose doc names this ticket as its owner. Or (b) it is NOT: `neq(a,b) <=> not(eq(a,b))` is a kernel law (stdlib/anthill/prelude/eq.anthill:28, spec §8.3 'the negation of the dispatched equality'), so a carrier-own `neq` is unexpressible-by-construction and should be a LOAD ERROR beside the existing AmbiguousEqDispatch/check_eq_noneq_exclusive refusals — which means revisiting WI-1098's row above, since that is the decision it recorded. (b) is the smaller change and the one the spec's own law argues for; (a) is what WI-1098's comment currently promises the user. ACCEPTANCE: whichever arm, the neq-only carrier must stop being silently structural on ALL FOUR prove_from_gamma consumers plus eval — drive each, do not infer; the two WI-755 floor rows either flip to assert the dispatch (arm a) or are replaced by a load-refusal row (arm b), and typing::guard_conjunct_reaches_undispatchable_neq + NEQ_OVERRIDE_SCAN_DEPTH_CAP + carrier_has_undispatchable_neq + value_reaches_undispatchable_neq are deleted either way; cargo-test green via scripts/test.sh."), acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test"), ToolPasses(tool: "scaland-sbt-test")], depends_on: some(value: nil), status: Open)

fact Tag(workitem: "WI-1125", name: "wi502")

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-1125", author: "user", content: "SCOPE ADDITION (from WI-755's second review round, all measured). Three sub-cases, not one — a fix must state its answer for each: (1) NEQ-ONLY carrier (own member OR instance-fact binding): nothing dispatches it, and WI-755's floor now suspends the guard route for it (typing::guard_conjunct_reaches_undispatchable_neq, reading spec_op_suppliers_for_carrier over PartialEq AND Eq, all three supply routes — a route-1-only read MISSED a 'fact PartialEq(T: Color, neq: colorNeq)' binding and dropped the effect; pinned by wi573_eq_override_discharge_test::fact_bound_neq_override_keeps_effect). The PROOF routes are still structural for it and that is this ticket's core. (2) BOTH MEMBERS, CONSISTENT (eq = false, neq = true): the resolver dispatches the eq and is RIGHT — 'neq <=> not(eq)' makes eq the authority. WI-755 deliberately does NOT block here, and the review's proposed one-line fix ('block whenever the carrier supplies its own neq') would have regressed it: MEASURED, restoring WI-573's blanket gate fails carrier_supplying_both_members_dispatches_its_eq. Whatever arm this ticket takes must keep that row dropping. (3) BOTH MEMBERS, CONTRADICTORY (eq = false AND neq = false, or eq = true AND neq = true): the carrier states something the kernel law forbids, the resolver silently answers via eq, and NOTHING CHECKS IT — not check_eq_noneq_exclusive (that is Eq vs NonEq), not AmbiguousEqDispatch (that is two eq suppliers). This is a missing LOAD-TIME COHERENCE refusal and it is the cheapest half of this ticket: a carrier supplying both members owes 'neq = not(eq)', and it can be refused at load beside the existing equality refusals regardless of which arm (a)/(b) is chosen for the dispatch question. Consider delivering (3) first — it is independent, small, and turns a silent wrong answer into a diagnostic.", at: "2026-08-17T17:07:44Z")
